# The terminal's insert mode (IRM), on lines of every size. CSI 4 h sets
# insert mode and CSI 4 l resets it (ECMA-48, IRM). In insert mode, each
# printed character pushes the text from the cursor to the line's right
# edge one place right, and the character pushed past the edge is lost.
# Nothing moves to the next row. On a line of double size the right edge is
# the line's last column, half the screen's width in columns, since each
# character there takes two columns. On a double-height pair, each line
# moves on its own, unless the program sends each character to both lines.
#
# Each step runs the typing pad, tools/vt/checks/typepad.py, with --insert,
# so F2 turns insert mode on and off and the key list shows whether insert
# mode is on. The insert screen holds a line of normal size, a wide line,
# an empty line and a tall pair, all but the empty line full of letters.
# One step runs the pad in pair mode, which sends each letter to both lines
# of the tall pair. make vt-check runs the guide from the checkout's top
# directory, so the steps name the pad by that relative path.
#
#   make vt-check GUIDE=insert

title: the terminal's insert mode on lines of every size

intro: Each step starts the typing pad. The pad shows the step's text at the
intro: top of the screen, then its keys, then the lines to type on. F2 turns
intro: the terminal's insert mode on and off, and the key list says whether it
intro: is on. Ctrl-D ends the typing, and then the step asks its question.
intro: In insert mode, each letter you type pushes the letters after the
intro: cursor one place right, up to the right edge. The letter pushed past
intro: the right edge is lost. The terminal never moves text to another row.
intro: A shell such as zsh draws its command line itself. When you insert a
intro: letter there, zsh writes the rest of the line again, so the text
intro: spills onto the next row. That is zsh's doing, not the terminal's. A
intro: wide line has letters twice as wide as normal, so its right edge
intro: holds half as many letters. A tall pair is two lines that show the
intro: top and bottom halves of the same letters.

step: Insert mode pushes letters right on a normal line
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: The cursor starts on the k of the first line. Press Left twice. Press
do: F2, so that the key list says "Insert mode: on". Type a few letters.
expect: Each letter goes in at the cursor and pushes the letters after it
expect: one place right. Each time, the letter at the right edge is lost.
expect: The wide line below stays as it was.
expect: Wrong: letters that replace the letters under the cursor, or letters
expect: pushed onto the next line.

step: A letter typed at the right edge
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Press F2, then End. Type one letter, then one more.
expect: The first letter takes the right edge, and the letter that stood
expect: there is lost. The second letter goes in at the start of the wide
expect: line below and pushes the wide letters right. No letter of the first
expect: line moves down.
expect: Wrong: the second letter on the first line, or the first line's
expect: letters on the wide line.

step: Insert mode on a wide line
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Press Down, onto the wide line. Press F2 and type a few letters.
expect: The new letters are wide. Each one pushes the wide letters after
expect: the cursor one place right. Each time, the wide letter at the right
expect: edge of the screen is lost.
expect: Wrong: a letter lost in the middle of the screen, or letters pushed
expect: onto the next line.

step: Insert mode on one line of a tall pair
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Press Down three times, onto the upper line of the tall letters. Press
do: F2 and type a few letters.
expect: Only the top halves of the letters after the cursor move, so the
expect: tall letters there look broken. The new letters show only their top
expect: halves. The terminal changes only the line the cursor is on.
expect: Wrong: both halves moving.

step: Pair mode keeps tall letters whole in insert mode
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --pair --insert insert
do: Press Down three times, onto the upper line of the tall letters. Press
do: F2 and type a few letters.
expect: The pad sends each letter to both lines. The tall letters after the
expect: cursor move right whole, and the new letters are whole too.
expect: Wrong: a tall letter whose two halves do not match.

step: With insert mode off, letters replace
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Press F2, then F2 again, so that the key list says "Insert mode: off".
do: Type a few letters.
expect: Each letter replaces the letter under the cursor. The letters after
expect: the cursor stay where they are.
expect: Wrong: letters pushed right.
